> Enter Your Mission Statement:	GENERAL HENRY CHAMPION TRUST, INC. is =
organized and operated exclusively for charitable, educational, and =
historical purposes within the meaning of Section 501(c)(3) of the =
Internal Revenue Code. The Trust is dedicated to the preservation, =
restoration, and protection of the 1790 General Henry Champion House in =
Colchester, Connecticut=E2=80=94an architecturally significant William =
Sprats=E2=80=93designed structure listed on the State and National =
Registers of Historic Places. The organization=E2=80=99s purpose is to =
restore the house and its historic landscape as closely as possible to =
their documented original appearance, including architectural elements =
described in the National Register nomination such as the gambrel roof, =
Ionic=E2=80=91columned entry portico, triangular=E2=80=91pedimented =
dormers with mutules, twelve=E2=80=91over=E2=80=91twelve sash windows, =
and the historic stone wall and setting. The Trust conducts historical =
research, architectural conservation, preservation planning, and public =
education activities that interpret the life and legacy of General Henry =
Champion, a Revolutionary War officer whose contributions are documented =
in the National Register filing. All activities are carried out to =
preserve this nationally significant landmark, expand public =
understanding of Connecticut=E2=80=99s architectural and =
Revolutionary=E2=80=91era history, and ensure that the property remains =
a cultural, educational, and historical resource for future generations.
> What is the charitable purpose of your organization?:	Environmental =
Quality, Protection, Beautification
> Describe Your Organization's Activities and Programs:	GENERAL HENRY =
CHAMPION TRUST, INC. serves the public by preserving and restoring a =
nationally significant historic property and making its architectural, =
cultural, and educational value accessible to all. The organization =
serves students, researchers, historians, preservationists, local =
residents, visitors to Connecticut, and future generations who benefit =
from the conservation of the 1790 General Henry Champion House and its =
documented architectural features. By protecting this State and National =
Register landmark and sharing its Revolutionary=E2=80=91era history, the =
Trust provides a public benefit to anyone seeking to learn from, study, =
or experience Connecticut=E2=80=99s architectural and historical =
heritage.
