43);">GENERAL HENRY CHAMPION TRUST, INC. is organized and operated =
exclusively for charitable, educational, and historical purposes within =
the meaning of Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code. The Trust =
is dedicated to the preservation, restoration, and protection of the =
1790 General Henry Champion House in Colchester, Connecticut=E2=80=94an =
architecturally significant William Sprats=E2=80=93designed structure =
listed on the State and National Registers of Historic Places. The =
organization=E2=80=99s purpose is to restore the house and its historic =
landscape as closely as possible to their documented original =
appearance, including architectural elements described in the National =
Register nomination such as the gambrel roof, Ionic=E2=80=91columned =
entry portico, triangular=E2=80=91pedimented dormers with mutules, =
twelve=E2=80=91over=E2=80=91twelve sash windows, and the historic stone =
wall and setting. The Trust conducts historical research, architectural =
conservation, preservation planning, and public education activities =
that interpret the life and legacy of General Henry Champion, a =
Revolutionary War officer whose contributions are documented in the =
National Register filing. All activities are carried out to preserve =
this nationally significant landmark, expand public understanding of =
Connecticut=E2=80=99s architectural and Revolutionary=E2=80=91era =
history, and ensure that the property remains a cultural, educational, =
and historical resource for future generations.</td></tr><tr =
